-- allium: 3
-- Opt-in approximate nearest-neighbour search over a table's vector column.
-- Table events and capture follow db.allium and allium/live-index.allium.
-- Generations are artefacts of kind `vector`: trie-cat.allium records them on trie entries, db.allium carries ArtefactsAdded through the leader, and gc.allium deletes them with their owner.
-- See rust/crates/vector, rust/crates/maintenance/src/artefact.rs and rust/crates/query/src/accelerate.rs.

entity VectorIndexDefinition {
    name: String
    relation: String
    column: String
    partition_columns: List<String>
    dimensions: Integer
    method: String
    recall: Decimal
    generation: Integer
}

entity VectorGeneration {
    definition: VectorIndexDefinition
    trie: String
    directory_digest: Bytes
}

entity ProbePlan {
    probe_fraction: Decimal
    shortlist: Integer
}

-- Definitions
--
-- `CREATE VECTOR INDEX name ON relation (column) PARTITION BY (columns) WITH (dimensions = n, method = 'ivf', recall = r)` declares a definition in `xt.vector_indexes`.
-- The generation is the declaring transaction, so a declaration after a drop has a fresh one and its artefact address.
-- `IF NOT EXISTS` leaves an existing definition and its generation in place.
-- Dropping a definition withdraws it at the ordered system cut; its generations become retired artefacts.
-- IVF is the only method.
-- The method name and the generation file layout participate in every artefact address.
--
-- Generations
--
-- A generation covers one live current trie and is a deterministic function of that trie's puts and the definition.
-- A put contributes its IID, valid interval, system-from, partition digest and unit vector.
-- Deletes, erases and puts whose vector has no defined cosine distance contribute nothing.
-- Rows sort by partition, IID and temporal coordinates before training, so their input order does not reach the bytes.
-- Each partition trains its own clusters with spherical k-means: evenly spaced seeds, a fixed number of rounds, f64 sums in row order and ties to the lower cluster.
-- A partition of at most 256 rows is one cluster.
-- Each cluster is an Arrow IPC stream aligned to 64 bytes; a directory names every cluster's partition, centroid, byte range, row count and digest.
-- The artefact record carries the directory's location and digest, and a reader checks every directory and cluster byte it uses against them.
-- The artefact lane builds generations for the tries `wants` admits and publishes them with ArtefactsAdded.
-- Compaction output gets its own generation; an erased version is absent from compacted tries and so from their generations.
-- A generation whose trie becomes garbage, or whose definition is retired, is deleted through the trie deletion grant like every other artefact.
--
-- The approximate read
--
-- A scan opts in with the predicate APPROXIMATE_NEAREST(column, probe, limit), which on a row holds where the column is not null.
-- Without a definition for the column, the scan evaluates the predicate row by row and ranks exactly.
-- The route applies only at a valid-time point and a system-time point, without transaction overlays.
-- The published frontier is the set of live current tries that carry a generation of the definition.
-- Tries above the frontier and the live segments are read exactly, up to a byte budget; beyond it the scan ranks exactly.
-- An equality filter that fixes every partition column selects the clusters of that partition before any ranking.
-- An IID scope from another accelerator small enough to rank exactly leaves the scan exact.
-- Each generation probes its eligible clusters nearest centroid first; the recall target sets the starting probe fraction and shortlist.
-- The shortlist keeps the nearest distinct IIDs by unit-vector distance across generations and the frontier.
-- The scan's verifier resolves the shortlist at the read cut and applies every predicate of the scan.
-- Fewer visible candidates than the limit double the probe fraction and the shortlist until the limit is met, every cluster and row has been considered, or the shortlist would exceed its budget and the scan ranks exactly.
-- The scan reads only the shortlisted IIDs, resolves their visible versions and computes the exact COSINE_DISTANCE on their stored vectors.
-- An erased, deleted or superseded version never reaches a result, because resolution at the read cut decides visibility.
-- A missing or corrupt generation leaves the scan exact.
-- Results of the approximate route may omit true neighbours; their distances and order are exact.
